{"items":[{"owner":{"account_id":2955660,"reputation":3414,"user_id":22377,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/fe2b7599d793fa92894c3c6f9c4e3b5a?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"verret","link":"https://mathoverflow.net/users/22377/verret"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1531256975,"post_id":304715,"comment_id":759886,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Do you want $S$ to be finite?"},{"owner":{"account_id":2940632,"reputation":19681,"user_id":431,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":58,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/0add74c2bd453b0e1189d5ba49ac421b?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"JSE","link":"https://mathoverflow.net/users/431/jse"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":2955660,"reputation":3414,"user_id":22377,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/fe2b7599d793fa92894c3c6f9c4e3b5a?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"verret","link":"https://mathoverflow.net/users/22377/verret"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1531257623,"post_id":304715,"comment_id":759890,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Totes.  I have edited."},{"owner":{"account_id":508162,"reputation":2320,"user_id":7092,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/100d8310b5bd242ea91d2f9e03e5848f?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Steven Stadnicki","link":"https://mathoverflow.net/users/7092/steven-stadnicki"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1531264731,"post_id":304715,"comment_id":759915,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"This has a lot of the feel of asking whether there&#39;s a single shape that tiles the plane only aperiodically, although clearly the two problems aren&#39;t strictly equivalent."},{"owner":{"account_id":2398579,"reputation":44581,"user_id":17064,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":55,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/b2a0a180cceac5d3dfa4e05b40f43992?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Gro-Tsen","link":"https://mathoverflow.net/users/17064/gro-tsen"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1531267949,"post_id":304715,"comment_id":759921,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I asked a particular case of this question <a href=\"https://mathoverflow.net/q/266915/\">here</a>, where $A$ is a Euclidean lattice and $S$ the set of Voronoi neighbors of the origin, I obtained a number of results in this are last year with Mathieu Dutour and Frank Vallentin which we are still in the process of writing down, but I hope to upload them to the arXiv soon.  All of our optimal colorings happen to be periodic but we don&#39;t know if there&#39;s a general reason."},{"owner":{"account_id":522930,"reputation":20431,"user_id":955,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":77,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/9103499995f2582c4eea6d0ce0f3eb0f?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"domotorp","link":"https://mathoverflow.net/users/955/domotorp"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":508162,"reputation":2320,"user_id":7092,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/100d8310b5bd242ea91d2f9e03e5848f?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Steven Stadnicki","link":"https://mathoverflow.net/users/7092/steven-stadnicki"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1531305467,"post_id":304715,"comment_id":759973,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Steven Why &#39;single&#39; shape? It more seems to me that the answer will be no, because there are tiles that only tile aperiodically. Also, I would rather reduce the Domino problem to the question: <a href=\"http://mathworld.wolfram.com/WangsConjecture.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">mathworld.wolfram.com/WangsConjecture.html</a>"},{"owner":{"account_id":14942180,"reputation":175,"user_id":132459,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/9579227ee1763e8933d0a719e8e34810?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Mike Krebs","link":"https://mathoverflow.net/users/132459/mike-krebs"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1602547689,"post_id":304715,"comment_id":946633,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"In the case of two-colorings, the answer is yes, and the proof is fairly straightforward.  Here&#39;s the idea.  Suppose c is a proper two-coloring, where the codomain of c is {0,1}.  For simplicity, assume that S generates A.  Without loss of generality, assume that c(0)=0.  (For if not, then reverse the colors.)  Then c is itself a periodic two-coloring.  If S does not generate A, then first restrict c to the subgroup generated by S, and then extend to all of A.  For &gt; 2 colors, this logic no longer works, and the problem seems to be much more difficult."}],"has_more":false,"quota_max":300,"quota_remaining":297}